<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Misima</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Misima is an island of the Louisiade Archipelago in Milne Bay Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a>. Gold was found there in the 1880s, attracting prospectors in the early days of British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a>, and a large modern gold mine operated on the island from 1989 to 2004.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/misima.jpg" alt="Misima" width="320" height="112" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Givet · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Misima_Island.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Misima, formerly called St. Aignan, is a volcanic island in the north-west of the Louisiade Archipelago, measuring about 40 by 10 km. It lies some 20 km north of the north-western end of the barrier reef of Vanatinai at Isu Raua Raua Island, and 80 km north-west of Vanatinai Island itself. The island is mountainous and densely forested, and Mount Koia Tau, 1,036 metres high, is the highest peak of the archipelago. The climate is tropical and humid, moderated by the north-east trade winds, with a dry and a wet season of about six months each.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Austronesian peoples have lived on Misima since about 1500 BCE. The French captain Louis Antoine de Bougainville sighted the island in 1768, and the French explorer Antoine Bruni d'Entrecasteaux explored it in 1793. The island owes its name to Élisabeth-Paul-Édouard de Rossel, a lieutenant on d'Entrecasteaux's voyage of scientific exploration. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> annexed Misima in 1888, and it became part of British New Guinea, later the Territory of Papua administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. Gold was discovered late in 1888, and by March 1889 eighty men were digging on the island and a storekeeper had set up in business.</p><p>Since 1975 Misima has been part of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a>. The modern gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mine was a joint venture of Placer Dome Inc, which owned 80 per cent, and the state-owned Orogen Minerals Ltd, and it gave the islanders much work. It was officially closed in 2004 because of its heavy load on the environment and the hazards it posed to public health. In March 2012 Barrick closed its post-closure monitoring office in Bwagaoia after rehabilitating the mine and mill sites.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>With 19,330 people in 78 villages, Misima is the most heavily populated island of the Louisiade Archipelago and the third largest by area, after Vanatinai and Rossel Island. Its main town, Bwagaoia on the south-east corner, is the seat of the Samarai-Murua District. The main language is Misiman, and many residents also speak English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tok-pisin">Tok Pisin</a>. Since the mine closed, artisanal gold mining has become a main source of income, with the Misima Alluvial Gold Mining Association founded in 2007, beside cash crops of coconuts, copra and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Misima Island” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Small gold rushes on <b>Misima</b> and Woodlark Island drew prospectors.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-new-guinea">British New Guinea</a></li></ul></section>
